# Elapsed time rules

This page explains active elapsed time, browser clock accounting, and display formatting in Neon Pairs.

`src/game/elapsed-time.ts` contains the formatter. Accumulation belongs to `advanceGame` in `game.ts`, and the browser decides which durations to supply through `session/use-session-clock.ts` and `session/browser-clock.ts`. Reading those three layers separately explains why rendering frequency does not define the measured duration.

## What counts as active

Elapsed time begins with the first valid reveal. Before that, `advanceGame` returns the existing state because `started` is false. Reading the controls or waiting at a fresh hidden board does not accrue game time.

Once started, time counts while the page is visible and the game is unfinished, unless an unfinished imported snapshot is awaiting Resume. It includes time spent looking at one revealed card, considering a choice between turns, and viewing a mismatch. It does not measure only the time when a pointer or keyboard is moving.

The browser running predicate combines four conditions:

```text
page visible
and session not paused
and game started
and game not won
```

The core knows only started and completion. Visibility and imported pause are browser-session concerns. Direct calls to `advanceGame` assume the caller has supplied an active duration.

There is no ordinary manual pause control during a played game. Resume is associated with loaded unfinished progress. Page visibility is based on `document.hidden`; the hook does not use window focus, pointer idleness, or the reproduction panel's open state as additional clock gates.

## Core accumulation

`advanceGame(state, milliseconds)` ignores a duration that is nonfinite or nonpositive. It also ignores it before start or after victory. For a valid active step:

```ts
const elapsedMs = Math.min(ELAPSED_MAX_MS, state.elapsedMs + milliseconds);
```

`ELAPSED_MAX_MS` is 359,999,999. The current implementation retains fractional milliseconds in memory, since monotonic browser observations can include fractions. It does not round each observation down before adding it.

The same call subtracts its delta from a pending mismatch. A large step can close the mismatch while adding the entire active duration to elapsed time. The 800-millisecond mismatch duration is not an upper bound on how much elapsed time that call can add.

Elapsed saturation does not end play. It also does not stop a mismatch countdown, because that countdown uses the original supplied delta. See [Mismatch interval](mismatch-interval.md).

## Observing the browser clock

`browser-clock.ts` stores one observation in an `ActiveClock`: `observedAt`. `createClock(now)` initializes it. `elapsedStep(clock, now, active)` computes the difference from that observation, returning a positive finite difference only when active is true.

Every result includes a new clock observed at `now`, even when no duration was counted. Advancing the observation during inactivity is essential: the next active observation can then exclude the period spent waiting, hidden, or paused.

The browser hook reads `performance.now()` and uses recursive timeouts with a usual 40-millisecond interval. It measures the actual difference between readings. It does not add a fixed 40 merely because a callback ran.

For example, active readings at 0, 0.3, 0.8, 1.1, 3.7, and 10.9 accumulate the same duration, within floating-point precision, as a single active observation from 0 to 10.9. `browser-clock.test.ts` checks this property, along with fractional durations and inactive intervals.

That example concerns logical observation, not a guarantee of a 40-millisecond browser paint. A busy task can delay a callback. When it runs, the measured difference catches up the game to the observed active time.

## Start, actions, and completion boundaries

Before processing a card activation, `useGame.activate` synchronizes the clock. At the first reveal, the game is still unstarted during this synchronization, so earlier waiting is discarded and the observation point moves to the reveal's time. The activation then sets started to true.

Later activations synchronize while the game is active. This captures duration between rendered ticks and ensures an overdue mismatch can close before a new card is considered. At the final activation, synchronization captures time up to that action before the game becomes won.

After victory, the running predicate is false and `advanceGame` also refuses additional duration. The completion time is retained without waiting for the completion banner's visual effect to finish.

Export also synchronizes before encoding a snapshot. Thus exported progress can be ahead of the last ordinary rendered tick, while still corresponding to the current active clock. It does not rely on a status string that was formatted earlier.

## Visibility boundaries

The visibility listener synchronizes before changing its stored visibility flag. On the transition to hidden, it counts the active interval up to that observation. On the transition back to visible, it observes the intervening duration as inactive, then resumes scheduling.

Suppose the game starts, runs visibly for 300 milliseconds, then a miss runs visibly for another 200. Hiding for five seconds adds nothing. On return, another 600 active milliseconds finish the mismatch. The total elapsed duration is 1,100 milliseconds: 300 before the miss plus the miss's full 800 active milliseconds.

Counting wall-clock time between first reveal and completion would include the hidden five seconds and disagree with the game's definition. A reproduction of a clock issue needs the visibility changes as well as the overall wait.

The hook clears its scheduled timeout while inactive. The implementation also handles a synchronization request made during inactivity by updating the observation without counting that period.

## Imported pause and resets

An unfinished import retains its elapsed value and waits paused. Resume resets browser observation before clearing that pause, excluding the time spent inspecting the position. If started is false, the clock still waits for a valid first reveal. Completed imports remain stopped by victory.

Successful replacements also reset observation. New Game and Restart receive zero game time from `createGame`; snapshots retain their imported value. Invalid loads do neither reset nor replacement. [New Game and Restart](new-game-and-restart.md) compares these operations.

## Display conversion

`formatElapsed` first floors milliseconds divided by 1,000. It then divides that whole-second count into minutes and a seconds remainder, padding the seconds to two digits:

```ts
const seconds = Math.floor(milliseconds / 1000);
const minutes = Math.floor(seconds / 60);
return `${minutes}:${String(seconds % 60).padStart(2, '0')}`;
```

| Stored milliseconds | Formatted value |
| ---: | --- |
| 0 | `0:00` |
| 999.9 | `0:00` |
| 1,000 | `0:01` |
| 59,999 | `0:59` |
| 60,000 | `1:00` |
| 3,600,000 | `60:00` |
| 359,999,999 | `5999:59` |

There is no separate hour field. Minutes continue past 59. `GameStatus` places the `Time:` label alongside this formatted value, and completion feedback uses the same formatter.

The formatter does not enforce the game's domain or cap its argument. It assumes a valid duration supplied by state. The cap is enforced by advancement and snapshot validation, so a direct unsupported call to the formatter is not an alternative game input path.

## Snapshot precision

`encodeSnapshot` floors elapsed milliseconds to a whole number. It rounds a pending mismatch remainder upward. These opposite rounding directions preserve the external constraints: elapsed is a whole nonnegative duration, and a still-pending mismatch has at least one millisecond remaining.

For an in-memory elapsed value of 11.75 and a mismatch remainder of 799.25, export writes elapsed 11 and remaining 800. Import restores those integer values. The live game keeps its fractions; export does not replace it with the rounded representation.

A round trip can therefore change submillisecond precision in the restored copy. That is expected at the format boundary. It does not justify rounding every browser tick, which would repeatedly discard fractions and make accumulated time depend on observation frequency.

The fractional browser test uses quarter milliseconds so its test values are exactly representable in binary. It checks both elapsed export and a final quarter-millisecond mismatch remainder exporting as 1.

## Replay uses explicit active time

Replay advances to each action's logical timestamp before activation, then to its end time. Waiting before the first valid reveal is ignored, and completion stops later advances. Hidden intervals and import pauses are omitted from this active timeline. Compare active durations when comparing replay and browser results; see [Replay format](../sharing/replay-format.md).

## Source evidence

`elapsed-time.test.ts` covers the cap, exported capped progress, and rejection above the cap. `browser-clock.test.ts` checks fractional accumulation and inactive observations. `tests/clock-edges.e2e.test.ts` exports between scheduled ticks. `tests/idle-clock.e2e.test.ts` checks untouched hidden and paused intervals.

These are distinct checks: a formatter example verifies text, a core advance verifies arithmetic, and a browser journey verifies which intervals reach that arithmetic. A correct-looking status string alone cannot prove that a hidden period was excluded or that the final click was synchronized.
# Scoring and moves

This page explains the pair score, the move counter, and the saturating move limit in `src/game/game.ts`.

Neon Pairs gives one point of progress per matched pair. Moves count completed two-card attempts. These quantities answer different questions: pairs show how much of the board is finished, and moves show how many attempts were used. Elapsed time is displayed alongside them but does not alter either value or decide the saved best.

The short contract is in [Rules](rules.md). Record comparison and persistence are covered separately in [Best score records](best-score-records.md).

## Where the numbers come from

`GameState` stores `moves` and an array of matched position indices. It does not store a separate numeric pair score. `GameStatus.tsx` derives the progress display as:

```text
matched pairs = game.matched.length / 2
total pairs   = game.deck.length / 2
```

A successful comparison appends two positions, so the displayed pair count increases by one. A miss appends none. A first reveal also appends none. The denominator is fixed by the board size for the life of that game.

A size-4 board contains 16 cards and 8 pairs. After matching positions 2 and 6 of `np1-4-1`, the status shows `Moves: 1` and `Pairs: 1/8`. A later miss raises moves to 2 while pairs remain 1/8.

No bonus is attached to a fast guess, consecutive matches, a large board, a first try, or a particular face. The pair count is enough to express progress. A saved best is the fewest completed attempts for that size, not the pair total, which is identical for every completed game of that size.

## Exactly when a move is charged

In `activateCard`, the increment appears in the one-card branch after the repeated-index check:

```ts
const first = state.selection.index;
if (first === index) return state;
const moves = Math.min(MOVES_MAX, state.moves + 1);
```

The function has already excluded invalid indices and matched positions. Reaching this calculation therefore means a second distinct eligible card has been revealed. The match and mismatch branches both use the same calculated value.

This placement makes counting independent of the comparison outcome. It also means the player is charged at the reveal, not when a mismatch later closes. During the 800-millisecond mismatch interval, the attempt is already included in moves.

| Operation | Move effect |
| --- | --- |
| First valid reveal from closed selection | None |
| Activate the same first position again | None |
| Reveal a different unmatched position | Add one, subject to cap |
| Activate a matched position | None |
| Activate during a pending mismatch | None |
| Advance time until a mismatch closes | None |
| Move keyboard focus | None |
| Start or restart a game | Reset the game counter to zero |

An imported unfinished session also blocks activation before the core is called. It does not charge moves for attempts to activate cards while paused. Resume itself changes the pause state, with no move increment.

## A worked sequence

Use the fixed deck `np1-4-1` to distinguish attempts from individual reveals:

```text
reveal 2          one card, 0 moves, 0 pairs
reveal 2 again    unchanged
reveal 6          match, 1 move, 1 pair
activate 6        unchanged, because 6 is matched
reveal 0          one card, still 1 move
reveal 1          miss, 2 moves, still 1 pair
activate 3        ignored while mismatch remains
advance 800 ms    mismatch closes, still 2 moves
reveal 0          one card, still 2 moves
reveal 11         match, 3 moves, 2 pairs
```

The two completed matches and one miss total three attempts. There are more activation calls because some were first reveals or no-ops. Counting event handlers, clicks, or key presses would not reproduce this number.

An ignored browser activation can still synchronize elapsed time first. That does not make it a move. Time advances and card activation are separate operations in the controller; [Turn resolution](turn-resolution.md) explains their ordering.

## Theoretical minimum

Every completed pair needs one successful second reveal. The lowest possible move count is therefore the number of pairs:

| Size | Minimum completed moves |
| --- | ---: |
| 4x4 | 8 |
| 6x6 | 18 |
| 8x8 | 32 |
| 10x10 | 50 |
| 12x12 | 72 |
| 14x14 | 98 |
| 16x16 | 128 |

The completion test groups positions by face and reveals each known pair consecutively. It reaches exactly these minima for every supported size. This is an oracle-driven rules check.

There is no par, grade, percentage, or efficiency multiplier in the current state or status component. A maintainer can calculate excess attempts as moves minus pairs for an ordinary unsaturated game, but the application does not keep that as another score field.

Even that calculation has a boundary: after the move counter saturates, it no longer reports the full number of attempts. The cap is a limit on the stored counter, not on how many turns the player can take.

## Saturation at 999,999

`MOVES_MAX` is `999_999`. A second reveal at 999,998 produces 999,999. A later second reveal keeps the value 999,999. The implementation uses `Math.min`, so ordinary progression does not wrap to zero or grow beyond the limit.

Reaching the cap does not end a game. Matching, mismatching, the pending interval, elapsed time, snapshot export, and eventual victory continue to work. A capped game can still have a first selection, a mismatch, or a closed selection between attempts.

For a match at the cap, both positions are appended to `matched` exactly as usual. For a miss, selection gets the same 800-millisecond initial remainder. Neither path substitutes an error or disables further input because of the move count.

A completed capped game can establish a record for a size that has none. It cannot beat a lower existing record. Equality retains the previous record. The stored score is the capped count; there is no hidden larger attempt counter used for comparison.

`src/game/move-cap.test.ts` checks reaching the cap with a match, finishing the remaining pairs, exporting and restoring completion, and resolving a capped mismatch. These tests address continued play directly rather than merely asserting the constant's value.

## Time and moves are independent

`advanceGame` never changes moves. `activateCard` does not add elapsed duration. A logical replay can match several pairs at the same timestamp, while a visible player can spend a long time with one card revealed and still have zero moves.

The two caps are independent. A saturated move counter does not stop active time, and saturated elapsed time does not stop second reveals from being counted. Completion depends on found pairs. See [Elapsed time rules](elapsed-time-rules.md).

## Imported counts and consistency

`decodeSnapshot` accepts a nonnegative safe integer move count no greater than `MOVES_MAX`. It also checks a lower bound based on the imported progress:

```text
minimum = matched positions / 2
        + 1 if a mismatch is pending
```

A pending mismatch has already consumed its second reveal, which explains the additional move. A one-card selection needs no extra completed attempt. A snapshot with one matched pair and a mismatch therefore needs at least two moves.

The decoder does not reconstruct every earlier guess. A large valid move count with little current progress can represent repeated misses. That is legitimate within the format's state checks. Validation establishes consistency, not an independently verified play history.

An unstarted snapshot requires zero moves, zero elapsed time, no matches, and closed selection. A started zero-move state cannot be closed; it has one revealed card under the other consistency checks. [Game state model](game-state-model.md) explains these cross-field relationships.

Saved best records use the same maximum. They also have a size-specific minimum equal to the total pair count, because a record represents a completed game rather than partial progress. A value of 1 for a size-4 best is invalid, even though 1 is a valid move count during that board's play.

## Reading a report about a wrong score

Identify the affected value first. Moves are stored by turn transitions; pairs are derived from matched positions; best comes from preferences. An uncounted activation may be a repeated position, matched position, blocked mismatch input, or saturated count. A pair display increasing by two suggests matched positions were shown without division by two.

For best lookup, `Game.tsx` uses `bestMoves[String(game.size)]`. A size change can select another record without creating a new score.

export const SelectionKind = { closed: 'closed', one: 'one', mismatch: 'mismatch' } as const;

export type Selection =
  | { readonly kind: typeof SelectionKind.closed }
  | { readonly kind: typeof SelectionKind.one; readonly index: number }
  | {
      readonly kind: typeof SelectionKind.mismatch;
      readonly first: number;
      readonly second: number;
      readonly remainingMs: number;
    };
import { createGame, MOVES_MAX, ELAPSED_MAX_MS } from './game.ts';
import { parseBoardCode } from './deck.ts';
import {
  hasExactKeys,
  isJsonObject,
  isNonnegativeSafeInteger,
  isWithinBytes,
  parseJson,
} from './saved-format.ts';
import { readMatched, readSelection } from './snapshot-selection.ts';
import { SelectionKind } from './selection.ts';
import type { GameState } from './game.ts';

export const SNAPSHOT_VERSION = 1;
export const SNAPSHOT_MAX_BYTES = 65_536;
export const SNAPSHOT_KEYS = [
  'version',
  'boardCode',
  'matched',
  'selection',
  'moves',
  'elapsedMs',
  'started',
] as const;

export function encodeSnapshot(state: GameState): string {
  return JSON.stringify(
    {
      version: SNAPSHOT_VERSION,
      boardCode: state.boardCode,
      matched: [...state.matched].sort((left, right) => left - right),
      // Active time keeps its fractions in play. Export writes whole milliseconds, and rounding
      // pending time up keeps a mismatch at 1ms or more.
      selection:
        state.selection.kind === SelectionKind.mismatch
          ? { ...state.selection, remainingMs: Math.ceil(state.selection.remainingMs) }
          : state.selection,
      moves: state.moves,
      elapsedMs: Math.floor(state.elapsedMs),
      started: state.started,
    },
    null,
    2,
  );
}

export function decodeSnapshot(text: string): GameState | null {
  const value = isWithinBytes(text, SNAPSHOT_MAX_BYTES) ? parseJson(text) : undefined;
  if (
    !isJsonObject(value) ||
    !hasExactKeys(value, SNAPSHOT_KEYS) ||
    value.version !== SNAPSHOT_VERSION
  )
    return null;
  if (typeof value.boardCode !== 'string' || !parseBoardCode(value.boardCode)) return null;
  if (
    !isNonnegativeSafeInteger(value.moves) ||
    value.moves > MOVES_MAX ||
    !isNonnegativeSafeInteger(value.elapsedMs) ||
    value.elapsedMs > ELAPSED_MAX_MS ||
    typeof value.started !== 'boolean'
  )
    return null;
  const initial = createGame(value.boardCode);
  const matched = readMatched(value.matched, initial);
  if (!matched) return null;
  const selection = readSelection(value.selection, initial, matched);
  if (!selection) return null;
  const state: GameState = {
    ...initial,
    matched,
    selection,
    moves: value.moves,
    elapsedMs: value.elapsedMs,
    started: value.started,
  };
  const minimumMoves = matched.length / 2 + (selection.kind === SelectionKind.mismatch ? 1 : 0);
  if (state.moves < minimumMoves) return null;
  if (
    !state.started &&
    (state.moves !== 0 ||
      state.elapsedMs !== 0 ||
      matched.length !== 0 ||
      selection.kind !== SelectionKind.closed)
  )
    return null;
  if (state.started && state.moves === 0 && selection.kind === SelectionKind.closed) return null;
  return state;
}
# Source audit notes

This page records what the Neon Pairs source-inspection tests check, how they discover files and where their evidence stops.

The audits run as Vitest tests under `scripts`. They inspect authored text and configuration rather than executing the application. Their value is fast, concrete feedback about a deliberately small codebase. Their regular expressions are visible and limited; a passing audit is evidence about those patterns, not a complete semantic proof of the architecture.

## File discovery

`scripts/source-files.ts` exports `sourceFiles(folder)`. It recursively calls `readdir` with directory entries, descends into directories and reads ordinary files as UTF-8. A link or special entry causes `Source contains a link or special file.` rather than being followed or ignored.

The helper returns path/text pairs. It does not apply `.gitignore`, infer TypeScript projects or skip directories based on a general exclusion list. Each test chooses a starting folder and filters the returned paths. This makes the scan boundary part of the test source.

The broad authored-TypeScript checks scan `src`, `scripts`, `tests` and `art`, then retain paths matching `.ts` or `.tsx`. The shipped-source check scans `src` and omits files whose names contain `.test.` or `.fixture.`. CSS and HTML can participate in that latter source-text scan even though the TypeScript-only checks exclude them.

## Packages and compiler projects

The first test in `source-audit.test.ts` parses `package.json` as unknown data and reads `scripts/package.fixture.json` as the reviewed expected versions. It narrows object shapes before using their fields. It asserts that the runtime dependency names are exactly React and React DOM, then compares expected package versions against the combined manifest maps.

It reads `package-lock.json`, finds the root package entry and compares that entry's dependency and development-dependency maps against the manifest. This catches disagreement at the root. It does not independently audit every transitive package or establish what code an installed package executes.

The same test checks `type: module`, the five explicitly enumerated strictness settings in `tsconfig.base.json`, the existence of the app/Node/end-to-end config files, the exact typecheck script and the `vitest run` unit command. It does not compare every compiler option against a fixture. [TypeScript settings](typescript-settings.md) records the full current configuration.

## File length and type escapes

The authored-TypeScript test removes trailing whitespace at the end of the text, splits on line endings and checks for at most 200 lines. The technology specification's 100-line aim is not the threshold in this assertion. A file at 180 lines can satisfy the test while still deserving a behavior-based split during review.

The test rejects diagnostic suppression markers for ignore, nocheck and expected-error forms. A second expression rejects the standalone token `any`, selected `as` assertions, dynamic import and require calls. These are text patterns, so comments and strings can trigger them too.

The assertion pattern catches an `as` followed by an uppercase name, an opening brace or selected primitive/unknown forms. It does not reject every possible TypeScript assertion syntax. In particular, the source uses `as const` for named value objects and literal arrays. Calling this a complete cast prohibition would misdescribe the implemented check.

The static-import patterns search for `import` or `require` followed by an opening parenthesis, allowing whitespace. The compiler remains responsible for actual syntax and symbol resolution. A text scan is useful for the repository convention precisely because its behavior is simple to inspect, but it is not a parser.

## Core-effect scan

The pure-core filename set is `deck.ts`, `game.ts`, `snapshot.ts`, `snapshot-selection.ts` and `replay.ts`. For non-test, non-fixture source with one of those basenames, the audit rejects listed browser/global markers and a Math random call marker.

The matching is by basename extracted from either path separator, not by a fully resolved module identity. A new file with one of those names elsewhere under `src` would also be selected. Conversely, a new differently named core helper is not automatically added to this set just because a pure module imports it.

This is an important coverage limit. The check does not traverse imports and propagate a purity classification. It also does not cover every current data-only helper, such as `saved-format.ts`, through that set. Review still considers the operations and dependencies of changed modules. [Purity guide](purity-guide.md) explains that architectural reasoning.

## Shipped-source scan

For all selected non-test, non-fixture files under `src`, the audit rejects known audio, canvas and network constructs and a pattern for a deck diagnostic property on window. It also rejects the text markers `fixture`, `face-sheet` and `test-results` in shipped source.

The exact constructs include AudioContext, `new Audio`, audio/canvas markup, a fetch call marker and XMLHttpRequest. This is narrower than proving that every conceivable request mechanism or diagnostic exposure is absent. It does not inspect minified output produced by third-party tools.

The generated-output complement is `tests/build-output.e2e.test.ts`, which reads the built HTML and checks the unwanted fetch and preload markers. The browser complement opens the artifact. These checks have separate inputs and failure meanings; [Offline delivery](offline-delivery.md) connects them.

## Export and ownership conventions

`source-conventions.test.ts` builds its own TypeScript file list over the same four authored folders. Its export regex recognizes line-start declarations of exported constants, functions, interfaces, type aliases and classes, with an optional async marker for functions. It does not attempt to enumerate all legal ESM export syntax.

Its named-import regex collects names inside braces, strips an inline type marker and removes local aliases. The test creates a set of imported names, then asserts that every recognized export name is present in that set. For recognized exported constants it also records a single owner by name.

This is a repository-wide name check. It does not prove that a specific import resolves to the specific export being checked. A same-named import can satisfy the set membership, and re-export syntax or a different declaration form can escape recognition. Those limitations explain why direct imports remain useful to a human reader.

The practical intention is legibility: avoid publishing helpers that no actual behavior uses and avoid ambiguous ownership of exported values. The implementation enforces a recognizable subset of that intention, rather than a full reference graph.

## Predicate names and test names

Another expression recognizes exported function signatures with an explicit boolean or type-predicate return. It checks that the name begins with `is` or `has` followed by an uppercase letter. `isValidSize`, `isWon`, `isJsonObject` and `hasExactKeys` fit this style.

Because the pattern is textual, inferred return types and more elaborate signatures can fall outside it. This is a naming check for recognized yes-or-no exports, not a general attempt to infer every function's meaning.

Test names are inspected in files ending `.test.ts`. The pattern extracts recognized `test` titles and rejects requirement-ID forms such as an uppercase letter followed by two digits. Assertions may still carry requirement references; the check is about human-readable titles. Template-literal titles are included in the recognized forms.

## Environment reads

The environment-read test collects `process.env` access using a simple dotted or bracketed identifier pattern. It compares normalized path/name pairs against a fixed list. The expected pairs are:

| File | Name recorded by the expression |
| --- | --- |
| `scripts/replay-cli.ts` | `INIT_CWD` |
| `scripts/replay.test.ts` | `npm_execpath` |
| `tests/browser-test.ts` | `EXECUTABLE_ENV` |
| `tests/browser-test.ts` | `NAMED_COMBINATION_ENV` |
| `tests/browser-test.ts` | `NPM_AGENT_ENV` |
| `tests/browser-test.ts` | `TEMP` and `TMP` |

For bracketed reads it records the variable identifier, not the string value held by that variable. The browser helper's constants correspond to executable selection, named-combination enforcement and npm's user-agent value. The test normalizes Windows separators before comparing the sorted unique set.

The expression does not model destructuring, aliases, computed string expressions or every possible environment access. It is a narrow check of the current authored forms. The fixed comparison makes accidental environment-dependent game behavior conspicuous within those forms.

## Interpreting a failure

A file-length failure names the file. An export failure names the unconsumed symbol or duplicate owner. A predicate failure names the function. The environment test gives a changed set of path/name pairs. These failures are most useful when read alongside the small regex that produced them.

A false positive in a comment is still a real failure of the textual test, but it does not demonstrate that the comment executes an effect. A false negative is also possible when syntax falls outside a pattern. Neither case justifies describing the audit as more powerful than it is.

Compiler checks, focused unit tests and browser journeys remain separate evidence. The source audits can establish that the manifest and root lock maps agree while a browser journey still fails, or that game transitions are correct while an authored file violates a convention. [Code review guide](code-review-guide.md) treats those as distinct findings rather than flattening them into one pass/fail label.

