docs/architecture/technology.md:4:Status: spec version 2.2.
docs/architecture/source-layout.md:94:`scripts/source-files.ts` recursively reads ordinary files from a supplied folder and rejects links or special entries. `scripts/source-audit.test.ts` inspects packages, compiler settings, source length, unsafe constructs and selected effect boundaries. `scripts/source-conventions.test.ts` checks recognized exports/imports, yes-or-no naming, test names and environment reads.
docs/history/meeting-notes-architecture.md:61:The review left future feature organization to actual feature work. Hints, themes, deadlines, and connected sessions all raise different questions; none justifies preparing unused state branches today. It also left broader browser evidence to explicit test work rather than inferring it from the named Chromium run.
docs/history/retired-idea-hints.md:5:The current game has no hint control, hint counter, assisted-result category, or hint-related snapshot field.
docs/history/research-memory-games-survey.md:53:This aligns with offline distribution as one HTML file and with a small React composition. It does not mean that single-page layout is universally simpler for every future feature. The [routing decision](../decisions/adr-044-no-routing.md) and [architecture page](../architecture/architecture.md) explain the current project's scale and boundaries.
docs/history/release-notes-1-3.md:52:With a named deal, the difference between New Game and Restart became concrete. New Game chooses a fresh seed at the current size. Restart uses the current code again. Both clear progress, but only one changes the starting arrangement. A page reload starts a fresh deal rather than secretly resuming the previous attempt.
docs/history/retired-idea-themes.md:65:If visual variety returns to discussion, these distinctions remain useful questions rather than inherited answers. [Roadmap notes](roadmap-notes.md) treat that as exploratory. The present game continues to use the dark treatment described in [Visuals](../visuals/visuals.md), with no hidden promise that a theme chooser is waiting behind the existing token names.
docs/gameplay/turn-resolution.md:133:Game replacement discards the current selection and increments session generation, so an old clock callback cannot close a replacement game's new card. Restart recreates the same hidden code; New Game requests a fresh seed. [New Game and Restart](new-game-and-restart.md) covers the shared replacement path.
docs/gameplay/deck-generation.md:137:At startup, the hook calls `freshBoardCode(preferences.size)` without a previous seed. The stored preferences contain the size and best move records, not the last deal's seed. Restart follows a different path: it calls `createGame` with the existing code and does not request a fresh seed. See [New Game and Restart](new-game-and-restart.md).
docs/gameplay/elapsed-time-rules.md:76:Successful replacements also reset observation. New Game and Restart receive zero game time from `createGame`; snapshots retain their imported value. Invalid loads do neither reset nor replacement. [New Game and Restart](new-game-and-restart.md) compares these operations.
docs/gameplay/gameplay-faq.md:43:## What is the difference between New Game and Restart?
docs/gameplay/gameplay-faq.md:47:Changing size also starts fresh progress. There is no collection of suspended games behind the size options. [New Game and Restart](new-game-and-restart.md) compares all replacement paths.
docs/gameplay/new-game-and-restart.md:1:# New Game and Restart
docs/gameplay/new-game-and-restart.md:49:Resetting that observation does not itself zero `elapsedMs`: New Game and Restart get zero from `createGame`, while snapshots retain validated imported time. Resume resets observation again before clearing pause, preserving all restored progress. A resumed unstarted snapshot still waits for its first valid reveal. See [Elapsed time rules](elapsed-time-rules.md).
docs/gameplay/best-score-records.md:124:Reload creates a fresh deal at the saved size, retaining the record map. New Game and Restart also retain the map while clearing progress. Successful loads select their game's size, changing which entry is visible; invalid loads retain the current size.
docs/gameplay/best-score-records.md:126:A completed import can therefore change the displayed best by selecting another size without establishing a record. [New Game and Restart](new-game-and-restart.md) compares these paths.
docs/gameplay/best-score-records.md:134:There is also no list of past attempts. The map contains at most one score for each supported size because each size is one key. It does not retain the second-best result, a date, a player name, or a streak.
docs/gameplay/board-size-catalogue.md:25:Four rows and four columns are the fallback when no valid saved preferences exist. There are eight faces, all from the first shape group: solid shapes with a top marker. Their labels use the cyan color name. Shape alone distinguishes those eight identities within this size.
docs/gameplay/board-size-catalogue.md:99:Switching options replaces the current game rather than keeping suspended games per size. [New Game and Restart](new-game-and-restart.md) compares these paths.
docs/visuals/visuals.md:4:Status: spec version 2.2.
docs/visuals/typography.md:60:A game card's face uses SVG geometry. It has no printed number, emoji or visible face name inside the button. `aria-label` names the position, state and visible identity for assistive technology, while the inline artwork is hidden from the accessibility tree. [Card artwork](card-artwork.md) explains that markup boundary.
docs/gameplay/rules.md:4:Status: spec version 2.2.
docs/visuals/theme-tokens.md:22:The page background has no `--page` custom property in the source. The visual specification calls the role Page, but the code expresses it as a root background literal. Similarly, the Focus role is implemented with a white literal in the global focus-visible rule, not a `--focus` variable. Role names in the specification and variable names in the implementation are related without being identical lists.
docs/gameplay/win-conditions.md:79:An unfinished import behaves differently: it is paused until Resume, and a later final match uses the normal activation path. That later completion can update the local best and set celebration. The distinction is covered in [New Game and Restart](new-game-and-restart.md).
docs/sound/feedback-without-audio.md:110:New Game and Restart replace the current session game through `replaceGame`. That clears the previous feedback and load notice, removes the celebration flag and increments the generation. The board is keyed by that generation in `Game.tsx`, which resets its remembered focus position with the replacement. The player sees a closed board and cleared counters rather than a reset sound.
docs/sound/audio-research-notes.md:39:The decision was to remove the sound treatment from this version rather than ship the prototype's control. That is why the current absence of a switch is meaningful. A developer looking for the mute state will find no audio preference waiting to be wired up. `Controls.tsx` exposes Grid size, New Game and Restart. `Reproduction.tsx` handles board and snapshot operations. Neither presents a sound option.
docs/quality/browser-test-guide.md:108:There is no automatic trace or screenshot-on-every-failure configuration in this wrapper. The presence of a screenshot from another case does not prove the failed case captured its final state. Artifact names and content are evidence created by their specific tests. [Accessibility audit](accessibility-audit.md) describes the normalized tree artifact and its limits.
docs/sound/sound.md:4:Status: spec version 2.2.
docs/quality/known-issues.md:48:The runner imports and launches Chromium. Some tests also use the Chromium DevTools Protocol for accessibility and touch input. There are no Firefox or WebKit projects in this source tree. The README records the named Node/npm/Playwright/Chromium set and says other versions have no test record there.
docs/competition/competition-pairing.md:30:In this scope, the seed identifies a place derived from entry. It is not an estimate of memory skill. The current game has no account history, participant rating or service from which to obtain such an estimate. A developer looking for an existing ranking calculation in `src/game` will not find one.
docs/competition/competition-scores.md:32:There are no participant names or board codes in that value. A best belongs to a board size across ordinary games, rather than to a particular generated deal. A person who sees the size-4 best is not seeing the result of the current board code unless the circumstances happen to coincide.
docs/competition/competition-scores.md:46:The solo functions still have no competition flag. That is intentional: the tournament controller owns a distinct state lifetime instead of threading an event mode through the solo session.
docs/competition/tournament-mode.md:15:Tournament games use the same active-time and card transitions as solo games. Opening Tournament mode suspends the solo clock, and tournament turns do not change the saved solo size or best moves. There is no backend or account identity; imported tournament data is validated as a consistent record, not authenticated as proof of play.
docs/competition/tournament-mode.md:23:Export tournament synchronizes the active clock before filling the Tournament JSON box. With no active event it exports the newest past tournament. With neither, it leaves the box empty and reports `Start or load a tournament before exporting.` Load tournament validates the box before replacing the event. Replacing an unfinished event asks for confirmation. A valid completed import is also added to the local past list.
docs/persistence/persistence-envelope.md:51:There is no migration chain in the preference store. An unsupported discriminator produces the same fallback as a malformed value. No code attempts to guess an older shape, rename its fields or convert a saved sequence into a best map. A migration requires a code change with observable effects.
docs/persistence/retention-policy.md:37:`replaceGame` always constructs a preference object with the new game's size. New Game and Restart consequently pass through a save-capable path even when the size value stays the same. Valid board-code and snapshot loads do so as well. Invalid loads update feedback without replacing preferences.
docs/persistence/storage-failure-policy.md:11:The preference adapter does not yet expose a blocked-status result. `readPreferences` returns a `Preferences` value in all cases, and `writePreferences` returns `void`. Neither return type carries an availability flag or failure reason. `Game.tsx` renders no preference-storage warning; saved-record features have separate failure feedback.
docs/persistence/storage-failure-policy.md:29:After a denied startup read, the game starts at the default size with no saved bests. Choosing another supported size updates the active session. Completing a game can produce a best for that size in memory. New Game and Restart preserve those in-memory preferences because `replaceGame` copies the session preference object rather than rereading the browser store.
docs/persistence/storage-quota-notes.md:48:There is no test that fills a physical browser storage area to its exact capacity. There is no recorded capacity sweep for Firefox or Safari in the test suite. The README reports Chromium journeys on its named compatibility combination; that is functional evidence for the game, not a general quota benchmark. An engineer should not attach a browser-specific measured threshold to these results.
docs/sharing/board-code-format.md:25:There is no checksum, title, player name or progress suffix. A typo can produce another valid code, so parser success cannot confirm that the sender intended that particular seed. The code is an identifier of generation inputs, not a signed statement about the game.
docs/persistence/private-browsing-notes.md:25:When `window.localStorage` throws during acquisition, `browserStore` returns null. `useGame` captures that value for its mounted lifetime. Reading returns default preferences and later writes through the null store do nothing. The selected size and completed bests can still change inside the session, so New Game and Restart continue to work with the current in-memory preferences.
docs/interface/input.md:4:Status: spec version 2.2.
docs/interface/tables-policy.md:31:There is also no ARIA grid role on the current board. Its native buttons and movement handler implement the specified interaction without declaring a separate composite widget role. [Board keyboard navigation](board-keyboard-navigation.md) traces that behavior. A review of result markup should not accidentally rename or restructure the card controls because the word “row” appears in both contexts.
docs/interface/announcements-policy.md:56:New Game and Restart call `replaceGame` with `imported: false`. That replacement clears the prior feedback and load notice while keeping the announcement number. A successful board-code load follows the same replacement path. These operations clear old context; the current source does not add a success sentence for a board-code load.
docs/interface/form-layout-guide.md:28:The snapshot textarea has no need for a FormData name in its current path because its value is held in React state and passed directly to `loadSnapshot`. Adding a name attribute alone would not turn its surrounding buttons into a form submission. [Native controls guide](native-controls-guide.md) explains the behavioral value of the current element choices.
docs/interface/control-bar.md:67:New Game and Restart still replace the solo deal immediately. Tournament opens the separate tournament screen; leaving an unfinished tournament uses its confirmation dialog. [Dialog policy](dialog-policy.md) describes that boundary.
docs/interface/error-display-guide.md:75:Name-related errors do not exist in the current component tree. Their policies are in [Name length policy](name-length-policy.md) and [Duplicate names policy](duplicate-names-policy.md). The load forms use their own messages and have no shared name-error component.
docs/decisions/adr-023-results-as-lists.md:11:This was a presentation decision in the original design discussion; the current game has no ranking screen. The current playable implementation shows its own move count, pair progress, time and per-size best.
docs/interface/screen-reader-notes.md:5:The interface exposes native controls, card names, state changes and readable results. The repository README records accessibility-tree checks in Chromium 156.0.8078.4 with Playwright 1.64.1. It also explicitly records that no screen reader was run. The names and sentences below are source-backed content available to assistive technology, not a transcript of speech captured in a listening session.
docs/interface/focus-policy.md:7:## Existing page and future screens
docs/interface/focus-policy.md:25:New Game and Restart are native buttons that call the session controller. Neither callback moves focus into the board. After either action, a keyboard user remains on the button they used and can repeat the action or Tab toward the board. The size select uses its native interaction and likewise does not issue a board focus call.
docs/decisions/adr-041-name-limits.md:11:That choice belonged to an earlier interface design discussion. The game has no player-name entry field in its current control tree. Its text fields are a board code and snapshot JSON, with format-specific limits.
docs/decisions/adr-045-no-i18n-yet.md:25:A translation framework from the start would have provided extraction and lookup machinery but no reviewed translations by itself. Embedding placeholder catalogues could imply broader support than the product had evidence for. Relying on automatic browser translation would leave accessible names and exact instructional language outside the team's reviewed behavior.
docs/decisions/adr-044-no-routing.md:3:This record explains why the current game has no router, route state or navigation layer.
docs/decisions/adr-044-no-routing.md:13:New Game and Restart replace the game state and increment generation. Loading a board or snapshot validates text and then replaces the state. These transitions have clock and focus consequences, but they do not create URL history entries. The board code is an explicit field and format rather than a path segment.
docs/interface/text-entry-policy.md:71:Tournament names have no character allowlist or prohibited-name list. Adding one while applying spacing cleanup would be a separate product decision, not a consequence of Unicode normalization.
docs/interface/disabled-state-guide.md:59:New Game and Restart are available during ordinary play, mismatch waiting and imported pause. Their controller operations replace the game and clear the old session context. There is no busy-state branch disabling them while a timer is active.
docs/interface/disabled-state-guide.md:65:This describes the existing controls, not a new general rule that every possible action in a future feature is always enabled. The relevant question in review is which layer owns the reason an action cannot change state, and whether the element's semantics and focus behavior match that reason.
docs/interface/duplicate-names-policy.md:11:The scope is one group. Neon Pairs has no global name registry across devices, browser origins or unrelated groups. A local comparison can answer whether an entry repeats within the group being acted on without implying that the person owns a unique account name.
docs/decisions/adr-017-colocated-tests.md:33:A central unit-test directory was considered simpler to scan by test type, but it detached examples from the functions under review. The current organization favors the engineer opening one behavior directory to understand both implementation and evidence. It is consistent with the existing architecture and does not imply a new filename convention for future feature modules. [Fixtures guide](../quality/fixtures-guide.md), [Source audit notes](../architecture/source-audit-notes.md) and [ADR 051](adr-051-test-artifacts.md) provide the supporting details.
